AWS live (keep OpenSearch):

- Do not ship the interview stack (ECS Fargate + RDS + ElastiCache + Amazon OpenSearch Service). That is $150-250/mo for empty traffic. Amazon OpenSearch Serverless is worse ($175-700/mo idle). The catalog is ~20MB in Postgres and the search index is ~17MB. Idle CPU on the current stack is under 1%.
- Buy one Lightsail Linux box: 8GB RAM, 2 vCPU, 160GB SSD, 5TB transfer, ~$44/mo. Run the same Compose services as local: nginx, web, api, Postgres, Redis, OpenSearch. Leave OpenSearch Dashboards off in prod. GitHub `deploy.yml` already SSHs to a single EC2; Lightsail is that shape with a cap and transfer included. t4g.large is ~$49 plus disk and a public IP — same idea, more knobs, skip unless you need them.
- Why 8GB in one pool: live RSS is OpenSearch ~1.5GB, Next ~630MB, Nest ~580MB, Postgres ~75MB, Redis ~13MB, nginx+CDN ~70MB. Dashboards ~230MB stay off. Shared RAM is the point. OpenSearch heap is the freeze risk; it is safer with unused app RAM on the same box than on a 2GB sibling that swaps.
- CDN is CloudFront + S3, not a VM and not the Go CDN as the public origin. Pennies. That is the speed visitors feel. The Go resize/WebP path can stay as a builder/origin helper behind CloudFront, not as the edge.
- Do not split onto multiple smaller nodes for this traffic. Lightsail 2GB / 4GB / 8GB all have 2 vCPU, so a 4+2+2 split buys 6 vCPU you will not use and costs $48-60. OpenSearch on a 2GB node will OOM or swap (RSS already ~1.5GB). Give it 4GB and the split is $24+$24 before Postgres, for a ~0.3ms same-AZ hop that does not matter vs an OpenSearch query. Split later if one service actually eats the box, not in advance. If only two nodes were affordable, still do not put OpenSearch on 2GB; drop the split and keep the 8GB box.
- 4GB Lightsail ($24) is only viable if OpenSearch is dropped and search stays on the Prisma fallback. We are keeping OpenSearch, so 8GB.
- Home GPU/RAM (RTX 4060, 62GB): offline only — pre-encode catalog images, docker build, seed, local restore of `pg_dump`. Do not put the laptop on the request path (sleep, NAT, WiFi). This stack does not use the GPU on the hot path.
- Talk track if asked: this is the budget live topology. At real load, split API/web onto Fargate and search onto Amazon OpenSearch Service. Not before.


